"Stefan Monnier" <monnier@di.epfl.ch> wrote:
>
> I looked in the ld manpage and such but could quite understand how to make a .so
> library. I'm probably dense but still...
> 
> Anyboy could tell me what command to use instaed of 'ar rv' ?
> 
> 
> 	Stefan

Sure -- as you suspected, you use ld(1) to create shared libraries.
The manual will give you all the gory details, but here are some
simple examples:

Example 1 - create a shared library from two object files:

  ld -o mylib.so obj1.o obj2.o -shared

Example 2 - create a shared library from an archive:

  ld -o mylib.so -all myarchive.a -shared

  The -all flag tells the linker to incorporate all the modules in
the following archive, rather than just using those modules necessary
to resolve previously referenced symbols.  In example 2 (above),
if the -all flag were ommitted you'd end up with an empty shared
library.

For more than you ever wanted to know about shared libraries, see
section 3 of the "DEC OSF/1 Programmer's Guide", which is on
the Operating System distribution CD.   Section 3.5 deals specifically
with creating shared libraries.

