I don't buy this.  All the OS/filesystem bugs I've seen that would cause
a whole filesystem to be trashed are completely independent of the number
of files or the size; it's true that most of them (but not all!) are
related to the amount of activity, but even more than that, the type
of filesystem activity.

I think most unix systems have been robust against this kind of issue
for a number of years now; that is, the size of the root filesystem isn't
likely to affect whether it will come up single user or not.

