Maybe your problem is not connected to the hardware you have plugged in.

I had similar trouble on a DEC3000 running OSF/1 V2.0 with Motif
dying about once a week.  Usually, I could restart it from one of the
then borderless windows.  Since I use Linux at home I installed
fvwm-1.24l.  This is a resource saving yet flexible and virtual(!)
window manager (virtual screen is eg. 2 x 2 the size of the physical
screen).  Customizing it to look quite similar to mwm is possible on
the basis of example configs.  Anyway, from that time on I never ever
had more trouble of this kind.  It is even nice to have the same
.fvwmrc on the machine at home and on the office's alphas and Linux
boxes.

Note that, using cc (not gcc) I had to comment out

	/* extern int bzero(char *, int); */

from the file fvwm/alpha_header.h - it was already defined in
/usr/include/string.h for cc.  (Didn't went into looking for a "cleaner"
solution.)  Also, since fvwm uses color icons, it needs the xpm X-Server
extension available as xpm-3.4a from the archives.

