<gln@cs.simpson.edu> wrote:

Hi Gary,

This is a fun, but really silly, one.  We have pop and elm coexisting at
my site and this kind of problem (ownership of spools, etc.) hasn't been
an issue.

privs on my mail folder are:

-rw-------  1 paul     system    1799506 Feb 25 14:26 paul

We're running popper from UC Berkeley: 

UCB Pop server (version 1.831beta)

The wrench comes when people are doing elm and their pop client calls up
or if they've already read a stack of mail and then go into elm and find
1000 letters from the last month lieing in their in box.  These two ways
of handling mail don't coordinate so well with each other.

Another solution to this problem is to use the imap protocol instead of pop.
In this case you could use U Washington's pine for your unix client and
mailstrom for your macs.  What happens here is that both clients work on
the same files and folders.  The mac only down loads the text of requested
messages, (and marks them accordingly as read), otherwise it just
maimpulates the indexes which the unix client pine also reads.  It's
pretty smart.  There is a doc called imap vs. pop which is quite
informative.  There's also a newsgroup (plus FAQ) for pine.

Good luck,

Paul

