	Eric Mercer writes:
	
	# Thanks for the opinion, but how does it help me?  Please
	# keep in mind that the budget for computers at the CVRC is
	# tight & we rely on the rest of the university for support.
	# If the university is moving to OSF, then we don't have a
	# choice.

	# Now, let me say this again, I am FORCED to switch from VMS
	# to OSF/1.  We are running RDB, and we will probably switch
	# to Oracle.


	David Mathog replied:

	> Hard to believe that this move is going to save you any
	> money.  If you were using RDB under the CSLG, and can put up
	> with staying at the current revision level for the next N
	> years, it is FREE as part of the CSLG, which you probably
	> have to have anyway.  RDB for OSF/1 is not part of the CSLG,
	> nor will it likely ever be.


It is my understanding (and I could be wrong) that a university under
the CSLG program for a given operating system takes on a lot of the
responsibility for keeping CSLG running for a campus.  If a university
decides to drop, say, CSLG for OpenVMS, then it may not be easy (or
cheap) to keep getting this service for a single group.

At the Univ. of Wisconsin, CSLG/ESL is all administered through one
central group, which coordinates all of the license ordering and
access to CD-ROMs, plus acts as a "screening" service to preprocess
bug reports before passing them on to DEC.  At CERN, DECcampus
(European parallel to CSLG/ESL) is also centrally administered.

The net result: if a university drops support for CSLG for OpenVMS,
you may have a lot more work on your hands to keep this going for your
group (if it is even possible).  If this is the case, moving to OSF/1,
although a lot of work, may well be more cost effective in the long run.

My information is admittedly sketchy and based on my experience.  Does
anybody (DEC?) have better information?
							.oO Chris Oo.

