|>  2 calloc( 0x12003af3c, 0x1, 0x20, 0x20, 0x11ffff0d9)
|>	["..../src/usr/ccs/lib/libc/calloc.c":119, 0x12003fef8]
|>  3 bucketCreate( nHashTableEntries = 4096)
|>  ......
|>  7 main( argc=4, argv=0x11ffff868) ["../caget.c":71, 0x12001c128]
|>
|>(bucketCreate is in my source code, where calloc is called).
|>
|>
|>I infer from this that (dbx at least) thinks that calloc has been called with
|>5 parameters, which clearly isn't the case. The function is correctly
|>prototyped, so simple compilation errors shouldn't be an issue. Replacing the
|>parameter list by two variables according to the man pages (man 3 calloc)
|>gives the same result. Interestingly, the routine completes correctly (and as
|>far as I know, further program execution continues correctly) if the second
|>parameter is > 88(decimal), although it fails for any value <= 88.
|>

If all other things are equal at the point of failure, then the fact that 
the size > 88 does not break and size <= 88 does implies the free list is
trampled, probably by free()ing something you did not allocate or in some
other way trampling on bookkeeping data structures in the heap management.
I would guess that '88' is the upper-limit of a block size, i.e., allocations
bigger than 88 end up coming from a different free list.


