This is a GREAT topic so I thought it deserved its own thread. Really,
that other thread was going in too many different directions...

>> michaelb@hobbie.bocaraton.ibm.com (Michael Rogero Brown (Sys Admin) writes:
<snip>
>>when I hear things like IBM's plans on getting OS/400 to pass 1170,
>>it makes me wonder. [don't know anything about os/400, except that I
>>wouldn't think it Unix]

In <D4B088.3EtJ@austin.ibm.com> mbrown@austin.ibm.com (Mark Brown)
writes:
>> UNIX is supposed to be an "open" system,
>>that means a published set of APIs and behaviors that
>>*anyone* can implement.

UNIX really means more than a published set of APIs and behaviors.
It's a "family" of operating systems, with close and distant relatives
who, for status reasons, remain a pretty incompatible bunch. Each
"cousin" in the family has different strengths and weaknesses, some
are more mature than others, and therefore display different behaviors.
Others have learned to get along and are working on improving
relationships. However, personal identities cannot be lost in the
"family" so, in order for members to survive, each must have some
unique, attractive, and differentiating feature.

>If OS/400 (AS/400) can meet the published requirements... <snip> ...and
>apps written for UNIX can [port] and run on it with little or no change
>(the same state that different UNIX flavors are in today)....
>...then what is the problem?
>1170 covers the basic *commands*, so that shouldn't be it.
>It covers the basic header files and dev tools....
>
>Sys Admin is different? Big Deal. <snip>
>File systems different underneath? Competition came up with JFS (and
>others) -- let it continue unless you *want* to go back to "good old
>UNIX" hope-fsck-gets-my-files-back.

Implementation issues, such as file systems (aka "database"),
networking, spooling, etc. are not covered by SPEC 1170 (now part of
X/Open). Neither is System administration, by the way. How some UNIX
vendors implement certain features, and facilities for management
thereof, remain largely proprietary and not very "open."

Open standards, such as X/Open, identify basic services required for
interoperability and portability between systems, including those
outside the "family." There are many other standards that would
still need to be followed by a vendor to be "more open" (I do think it
is a relative term). These would have to include file services, data
access, data exchange, networking, mail services, and language
portability (beyond "C", c'mon!).

>Folks, that fact the systems like the AS/400 are moving towards UNIX
>APIs is a *win*win*win*.
>

After working for a long many years in the Unix industry, I'm currently
responsible for establishing a development and support environment for
applications that run on Unix-based systems, as well as on AS/400s.
Contrary to popular trends, several major Unix vendors are "porting"
their wares TO the AS/400 environment (another survival tactic and
differentiating feature for the bolder members of the "family", you
see).

IBM's strategy to place the AS/400 in an open, competitive,
client/server environment should not be overlooked. As the new release
of OS/400 moves closer to support Unix APIs (standard
libraries for "C", file I/O (yes!), sockets, threads, memory...) it will
be a *win*win*win* for *users*developers*vendors* (especially IBM...)

By the way, does anyone know if 90% compatibility with X/Open can make
my o/s "UNIX"? How about 80%? 75%?...

Cheers!

N. Diaz                       |<--  Openness is a state of mind  -->|




