Hard to believe that this move is going to save you any money.  If you were
using RDB under the CSLG, and can put up with staying at the current 
revision level for the next N years, it is FREE as part of the CSLG, which
you probably have to have anyway.  RDB for OSF/1 is not part of the CSLG,
nor will it likely ever be.  To date it has been impossible to get
academic prices for RDB out of Oracle (attention SEC!!!) but assuming that
they come out the same as for Oracle, you're looking at at least $2000/year
for an 8 user development system.  Ok, cheap by industrial standards, not
so cheap by academic standards.  Sybase was less, roughly the same amount
for the first year, much less for subsequent years. 

If you have to blow out of VMS you should really consider going to WNT. 
There are a bunch of databases available for that.  I don't know what kind
of data you've got, or what your other needs are, but likely you don't
really need all of the oomph of RDB, Oracle, or Sybase, and could probably
get by with something a bit lighter, and it would cost less than Oracle. 

Just a thought,

