In article <3iaau1$4tu@nrcnet0.nrc.ca> rao@autsrv.iitsg.nrc.ca (Rao Palacharla) writes:

>I am planning to install the DEC X.25 software (which includes
>support for ISDN) on the DEC ALPHA  3000 Model 800 workstation
>running OSF/1. The DEC alpha has a built-in ISDN card.  
>It seems (this is what the installation manual says) that ISDN 
>support is for two profiles - AT&T 5ESS and Euro profile. What we 
>have in Canada is Northern Telecom DMS 100 (compatible with NI-1 
>standard).

>In addition, how are these switch profiles characterized? 
>Are the profiles a set of parameters which are different 
>for each switch? In that case, I can just change those
>set of parameters to work with DMS-100.

It's more than that.  X.25 has "profiles" which can be quantified.
In DEC products, there's a procedure called "FCNS" in which a new
profile can be created.  (It's not trivial but it has been done dozens
of times.)  ISDN, on the other hand, has literally different protocols.
While NT-Custom and its near-clone NI1 are "based upon" CCITT (ITU)
Q.931, they have a lot of uniqueness.  Likewise, AT&T Custom has a 
lot of its own features, mostly in "codeset 6", which is where ITU has
room for vendor extensions.  

So the vendor has to really write different code for it to work.  Most
of it is common, and sometimes it's run off of a common code base,
but it's not as trivial as X.25 profiles.  Given a limited budget, DEC
chose to implement the two most widely-used flavors of ISDN (for
1995), which are AT&T Custom and Euro.  (At least I think those will
be most-used.  I admit to having played a role in that decison, back
when I worked there.)   I expect NI1 (DMS-100) support to be in a
future release, but I have no firsthand knowledge of such plans;
it's just logical (if the product continues).

>I was wondering whether anyone is using the DEC Alpha's with
>DMS 100's. If so, I would appreciate to hear from them.

I seriously doubt there are any (unless using external TAs).

Since certain DECees are known to read this group, perhaps they can
comment, IF they are authorized to give a "statement of direction" or
whatever kind of non-committal wording they use nowadays.  (Unlike
certain other large software companies I won't  name, DEC is pretty
good about not doling out vaporware.)



