I second that.  After just recently installing several workstations
I would rather install an Alpha any day of the week.  I noticed that
Solaris 2.4 had 75 patches already.  Not only do I have to figure out
which patch fixes which problem, now I have to know which patch does
not work with that other patch over there.  I think that Sun should
have their Solaris developer's/programmer's take turns installing the
software with the necessary patches.  Patches that fix things and do
not break other things.  Then maybe they would really fix the problems
and I would not have to work overtime installing their machines.
I hate working overtime & I hate patches!!!!!!!!!!!!

I installed the Alpha and had everything up and running in 2 hours.
The Sun's, well they still do not work quite right.  I probably should
have installed patch 999999-99 and not installed patch 666666-66.
This is the story I hear from Sun's service line.  Well needless to say
surely this OS will become stable over time (didn't I hear this last year,
and the year before, etc...).

On a more positive tone:
In my opinion Sun's 4.0 C++ compiler, C compiler and SPARCworks are
the best in the industry.  Last year we bought Dec's compilers and
they have some serious problems. The 1 year software updates are almost
up and they have not even fixed a basic problem.  Basically we wasted our
money buying the compiler.  We do all of our software development on
SPARC 20's.

These opinions are about the only things that are mine.

