AFSAR@jaguar.litc.lockheed.com writes:
>what I 
>am looking for is when you log in to system you get this message that says:
>
>OSF/1 (your host name) (#ttyp2)
[..]
>The file name for this kind of annoncements are found on Cray and Convex 
>systems in /etc/issue and I want to know what is file name in OSF1 systems.

Same for Linux.

On OSF/1, at least for the console, this is an issue of getty :-)
See gettydefs(4) for the definition of the login prompt on the console.

If you log in via rsh or rlogin, you don't get a login banner at all.
(just "login:" and "Password:" - if the client is not in the .rhosts
mechanism)

If you do telnet, the login banner is an issue of the server's telnetd.
Looking closely via "strings /usr/sbin/telnetd | grep /" reveals
(among other things):

OSF/1 (%h) (%t)
/etc/gettydefs

That is, your quoted line above is hard wired in telnetd.
I haven't looked into it further.  Maybe you can manipulate /etc/gettydefs
to force telnetd to give another banner.  Note that telnetd does seem to
NOT call getty but instead interprete gettydefs itself.
See getty(8), login(1) etc.


Hope it helps, Michael
