	  36     262    3963

This, of course, doesn't include the recommended patches that I suppose
you're referring to from one of the patch servers.

I think there has been a serious degradation in the quality of release
notes in recent years.  Once it was useful to read the release notes
and installation instructions.  More recently, these seem to be devoid
of much useful information, and instead take about 30-50 pages to say
"boot from cd and answer the questions".  This is a big improvement
from the early days of installation when one had to remember which
file on the tape contained the mini-unix, but the three recent solaris
installations (2.2, 2.3, and 2.4) did not make any functional
improvements on the basic interface, they just changed it a bit.

For instance, one of the first questions which confronts someone attempting
to install Solaris is, how big should the partitions be?  Well, the 2.4
release notes suggest something like running du on the release cd to answer
this question.  This is worthless.

Another example of poor installation guides are the hardware notes that
came with recent sparc 5's and sparc 20's.  There were four booklets, with
very similar titles, each in about five languages, evidently containing
about the same information, most of it not very interesting.  

The answerbooks and SunSolve are quite nice, once a system is up and running. 
However, I think something more productive could be done with the paper
documentation which comes with new software and hardware to make it 
both shorter and more useful.

-- danq

