: 	with V.42bis, dialback and RTS/CTS hardware flow
: 	control configured on. The cables between modems and
: 	alphas are 25-pin flat cable with compressed D25
: 	connectors; all needed RS-232 signals are sure connected.
: 	DTE speed between modems and alphas is set to 38400bps
: 	at both ends. OS is DEC OSF1 3.0.

: 	All works perfectly, *except* flow control. When I
: 	try to upload/download anything with kermit, all I get
: 	is timeouts and too many retries. If I type 'cat' in
: 	shell, and try to copy/paste a 60-char line from
: 	another xterm, the line will be garbled. The serial
: 	ports and/or OS on alpha seems to not handle 38400bps
: 	serial rate very well.

: 	I studied stty manual page on our DEC, HP and Sun
: 	and the only one which did NOT mention *anything*
: 	about hardware flow control was... OSF1.

: 	*** Is there any way to have RTS/CTS flow control? ***

: 	Currently we cannot transfer any files with our
: 	modem connection. I did fiddle with kermit parameters,
: 	but nothing seems to help. This stinks!

: 	I am very grateful for any help. Please reply or followup,
: 	whichever you find more appropriate.


I think that flow control between the modem and a computer is
determined by request from the modem. Here is my justification.
I also have a DEC Alpha 3000/300 running OSF3.0 and a MultiTech
2834BA modem.  When the first time I connected my modem with
my Alpha with two D25-D9 cables,  the login prompt and anything
output were garbled.   This is a sure sign that the flow control
from the modem is not right.  I then changed the modem setting
(from the default RTS/CTS) to XON/XOFF,   the garbled character
problem was gone and I could log in!   I then changed to a D25-D25
straight through cable  and set back the flow control on my modem
to RTS/CTS.   Now, everything is fine.

So,  see, OSF3.0 has nothing to do with above flow control problem.
It is my modem's problem.

Are you sure that your modem is set right?

