In <id.D8EH1.K7K@nmti.com> peter@nmti.com (Peter da Silva) writes:

>In article <3hqv3e$qb2@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>,
>Steve Peltz <peltz@cerl.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>
>> This is actually
>> a macro expansion, where in the general case it needs to look like a single
>> expression (it might be a macro expansion, or it might be a function call).
>
>It is considered bad form to pass an expression with a side effect to a macro.

It's considered even worse form to write macros that evaluate their
arguments more than once.  ANSI/ISO C requires that any macro defined
in a standard header (an implementation is allowed to provide macro
replacements for any standard function, provided that the function is
available in the standard library, too) evaluates its arguments only once.
Two exceptions: getc and putc (only the second argument of putc may be
evaluated more than once)

