rhkjcrp1@is1.hk.super.net (Mr Ka Sing Au) wrote:
>     1. When I used secsetup to toggle the security level back to 'BASE',
>     it rewrote /etc/passwd and stores encrypted passwords there. It is
>     normal.
>
>     2. Then I did the same thing and brought the security level to
>     'ENHANCED' again. This time it didn't change /etc/passwd, i.e.
>     encrypted passwords were still in /etc/passwd, not the auth. database.

I believe that when you shift from base to enhanced security, the passwords are
copied from the /etc/passwd file to the auth database, without removing them
from the /etc/passwd.  Subsequent chances to the password would only be
reflected in the auth database, IAW, the passwords in the /etc/passwd file
will become invalid, a nice way of messing with the mind of somebody who
cracks that file, but probably not a good idea on a system with a lot of users
who may not use passwd much.

You may find that when users log on to the system after changing to enhanced
security that they will be told they have to change their password.  At least this
is what happened to me when I borrowed a passwd file from another system
as a way of creating a lot of accounts in a hurry.  In this case, our security
problem is limited to those users who don't log on at all.  Fortunately, we
initially generate random character strings from passwords so crack isn't
going to have much luck.
.

Paul D. Chapin
