Steve Peltz <peltz@cerl.uiuc.edu> wrote:
> In article <id.M3CH1.QJF@nmti.com>, Peter da Silva <peter@nmti.com> wrote:
> >This is actually undefined behaviour for ANSI. All the comma operators
> >guarantee is that "temp = a + b" will be evaluated before "temp * (a + b)",
> >and similarly for the second expression.

> Is this true for an argument list as well?

The commas in argument lists are not comma operators and do not generate
a sequence point. For example:

	i = 1;
	printf("%d, %d", i++, i++);

Might generate:

	1, 2
	2, 1
	1, 1

(since this is "undefined behaviour" it might also send dirty pictures to
 clinton@whitehouse.gov)

> Is there any way to force the order for something like this?

Generate multiple temporaries.

> This is actually
> a macro expansion, where in the general case it needs to look like a single
> expression (it might be a macro expansion, or it might be a function call).

It is considered bad form to pass an expression with a side effect to a macro.
You may not be able to use macros in this case.
