] > 
] > Get this too:  if prog is running and I recompile prog  giving the new
] > executable a new name (cc -o xxx prog.c), prog doesn't crash.
] > 
] > Why should overwriting the executable on disk crash the corresponding
] > active process in memory?  Is this some weird sort of dynamic execution
] > thing that I don't know about?  

] Some unixs swap in the read-only parts of a running process from
] the executable file, rather than the swap slice. It looks like this
] one does so from what you say. [...]

A simple workaround is to "rm prog" (or "mv prog prog.running") before
compiling.  This ensures that the compiler creates "prog" with a
new inode and your running "prog" won't crash.

joerg

