Hi netters ,

   I am porting an application from Ultrix  to DEC OSF/1.
   This application involves two machines (in this case
   two DEC AXP OSF/1, one running V2.0 and other V3.0)
   talking on async device. Lets call them Primary and Standby.
   Their role is interchangable.

   When Primary process wants to query the status of Standby it sends
   a single character on async device /dev/tty00. This device
   is physically connected to /dev/tty00 of Standby.

   Processes on both machines executes following code to enable
   themselves to receive SIGIO:
   .............................................
   if(fork() != 0) exit(0);
   .............................................
   /* create a new session, make the calling process, a session leader
       and a process group leader */
    setsid();
   .............................................
   fd = open("/dev/tty00", O_RDWR);    /* open device */
   ioctl(fd, TIOCSCTTY, 0); /* Set the device as controlling terminal */
   ioctl(fd, TIOCGETP, &tty_mode);  /* get parameters */
   tty_mode.sg_flags |= RAW;  /* no processing of chars */
   tty_mode.sg_flags &= ~ECHO;  /* no echo of chars */
   ioctl(fd, TIOCSETP, &tty_mode) != ERR)  /* set parm */
   .............................................
   ...ioctl() /* set baud , char. length etc */.
   .............................................
   fcntl(fd, F_SETOWN, getpid()); /* enable SIGIO signal to be
                                     delivered to the process */

   Standby process reads incomming chars in SIGIO handler, and
   responds back on the same async device. The process on Primary
   reads that char again in SIGIO handler.

   NOW THE PROBLEM:

   I  monitored SIGIO by putting diagnostics in signal handler.
   and found that process on OSf/1 V3.0 randomly looses SIGIO signal.
   Is it because I am using different versions (2.0 and 3.0) of
