In article <3houb5$8cr@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>,
Steve Peltz <peltz@cerl.uiuc.edu> wrote:
> Expression such as:
> 	(temp = a + b, temp * (a + b)) + (temp = c + d, temp * (c + d))

> do not compile correctly with optimization level greater than 0. It uses
> the same value of temp for both multiplies, instead of correctly handling
> the parenthesized comma separated expressions.

This is actually undefined behaviour for ANSI. All the comma operators
guarantee is that "temp = a + b" will be evaluated before "temp * (a + b)",
and similarly for the second expression.

It is legal for the compiler to evaluate this as:

	temp = a + b;
	temp = c + d;
	... temp * (a + b) + temp * (c + d);
