Jon Jenkins (jenkinsj@flotsm.ozy.dec.com) wrote:
: Jon Jenkins (jenkinsj@flotsm.ozy.dec.com) wrote:

the latest news on the PC-VGA->RGB converter!!

Well we now have a working circuit and without
destroying video cards or monitors !!! The picture
is not perfect but putting 70-100MHz thru a breadboad
and several feet of breadboard connectors/wires
is as good as reason as any !!! The sync pulses
look fine and the monitor syncs up well both
in Windows and XFree 3.1 in all resolutions
I have been able to test.

Three big changes made the difference:

1: we have gone to a single +5V supply

2: we are using an N channel enhancement mode
   MOSFET as the sync switch

3: Use good quality ceramic or silvered mica
   for the capacitors

 So the upated circuit looks like this:

                       +5.0V
                        |
                        /
                        \ VR1 (~500)
                        /
                        \
       2x.047 ceramic	|   ~0.3V DC
 GREEN-----------||------------------------------------------------GREEN to workstation
               |                                  _|
  HSYNC--------|-------\                         |
               | OR    |------CSYNC--------------|    VN10KM or similar small 
  VSYNC--------|-------/                         |_   N channel enhancement MOSFET
               | 74HC32                            |
 						 Gnd
*HSYNC, VSYNC and CSYNC all tied to ground with 4.7k carbon resisitors
*HSYNC, VSYNC are +ve logic (i.e. sync=+5.0V), For -ve logic (i.e. sync=0.0V)
 the 7HHC32 will have to be changed to a NAND gate

Further improvements:

1: make up on a circuit board with short leads and shielded 75 ohm cables
2: get a higher frequency (smaller) MOSFET as the VN10K is a 1W power switch

