In article <3guknb$dk3@werple.mira.net.au>,
John Birrell <jb@werple.mira.net.au> wrote:
>standard diskless configuration that swaps across the network would
>tolerate the network going away. We need to hide the network recovery 
>from the users.
>
>Does anyone know if this is possible with DEC OSF/1 V3.0? Any
>assistance would be greatly appreciated.

I *do* know that it is possible to this kind of things using DEC OSF/1
2.x, so it ought to be possible using 3.0. In fact, it's probably far
easier to do it under 3.0, since you don't have to reinvent those
parts that DEC didn't deliver/support with 2.0.

A friend of mine did this for 2.0 (the aim was to do seamless network
install, just boot against the ethernet and it does the rest for you).

My memory of this is a bit hazy, but I think the main thing that was
needed under OSF/1 2.x was a virtual memory filesystem (a custom
device-drive, fairly small) used for the root filesystem and some
changes in the boot-scripts in OSF/1 2.x (to make OSF/1 2.x able to
data-less boot, there is a file describing this and I have seen an
article somewhere too)

It ought to be easier to do this using 3.0 than 2.x, since 3.0
supports use of a remote /usr. I don't know if you can supply a custom
root-filesystem (RIS seems to be able to do this, but it's possible
that it isn't provide so other can use it), but it should be possible
to use the same kind of memory filesystem otherwise.


