>The dataless configuration supported by DEC OSF/1 Version 3.0 uses a local disk
>for swapping while having the other file systems remotely served.  It may be

The swapping ought to be cured if you remove /sbin/swapdefault from
the root filesystem it uses. Without that file, and no swap
filesystems in /etc/fstab it shouldn't swap at all (if you exceed the
memory available it will start killing processes, but...).

Does it get the root filesystem from ethernet too? (seems likely) 
Can you modify the root filesystem? (if not, stupid design!)

If the answer is Yes to both these queries then it shouldn't be that
hard to do a truly diskless system under OSF/1 3.0 (if you have a lot
of main memory). If you then add remote swapping you get real diskless
machines :)


