Ramana Turlapati wrote in article <1733EC53BS86.UC577050@mizzou1.missouri.edu> :
>
>Hello all,
>
>I have a process that shows status "U" - stands for uninterruptible sleeping
>process, when I execute the command "ps -u USERID".
>
>How do I kill this process? I tried sending SIGABRT, SIGKILL, SIGINT etc
>to the process but it doesn't vanish. I would also like to know what causes
>this type of process to ignore all the signals.

You can't.

The kernel has deliberately put that process to sleep at an
uninterruptable priority.  The kernel should only do this
for processes waiting on events that have a very high likelyhood
of occurring.  Occasionally you will find these 'stuck' processes
but usually it's do to a logic error in the kernel, or a hardware
error.

Send me e-mail if you want to debug this further.

