In article <matap-090295165458@guardian.maths.latrobe.edu.au>, matap@lure.latrobe.edu.au (Andrej Panjkov) writes:
|> Hi--
|>
|> On an ALPHA 3000/300x with DEC OSF/1 3.05, this strange thing happens:
|>
|> Suppose 'prog' is the executable form prog.c.
|> Suppose prog is running.
|> Compile prog.c in another window (cc -o prog prog.c).
|>
|> When cc finishes, the excuting copy of prog crashes with a memory fault!?
|>
|> What th'...?
|>
|> Get this too:  if prog is running and I recompile prog  giving the new
|> executable a new name (cc -o xxx prog.c), prog doesn't crash.
|>
|> Why should overwriting the executable on disk crash the corresponding
|> active process in memory?  Is this some weird sort of dynamic execution
|> thing that I don't know about?
|>
|> Please explain this to me, and suggest a fix/workaround.
|>

The reason is that the part of the executable that is not in physical memory
is not somewhere else in virtual memory, but only on the disk from where
you startet it. So when you overwrite the executable, the program must
crash. (This is not the case if the executable is on a NFS-mounted disk!)
However, it is somewhat strange that it is possible to overwrite
a running executable. On Ultrix, this was avoided by a mechanism called
'file locking'.



