 Yes. 

We are evaluating the TTi (Transitional Technology inc) variant of the
DLT range (TTi2000). The drive has a nice front panel (LED display of
error rate, tape left etc.) and a personality module to emmulate Exabyte,
TK50Z and IBM RS-600.

I am impressed with the drive but not so with the advertised
performance. 

The literature (both DEC and TTi) quote a max throughput of 1.25MB/s
native and 2.5MB/s compressed. 

I have tried the drive on a DEC3000/300 (OSF/1 V2.1), DEC3000/400 (OSF/1
V3.0) and a SPARC IPC (Solaris 2.4).

On the 3000/300 I could only get a max of some 700KB/s to the drive
(compressed) and suspected a congested SCSI bus so I put the drive on
the external SCSI on the 3000/400. The internal SCSI had an rz26 and an
rz28 on it and the only device on the external was the DLT.

I observed a maximum throughput of 1.5MB/s using dump/dd/tar/cpio in
compressed mode (NOT 2.5MB/s). 

Suspecting "foul play" I used tapex to test the throughput of the drive
and low and behold I achieved 2.8MB/s Compressed and around 1.25MB/s
Native. Tapex (as far as I gather) writes directly to the drive.

The limiting factor is the filesystem/device drive interaction. If you
can get data to the drive fast enough then you can get the advertised
throughput. However, any real application (dump/tar etc) is limited by
the filesystem.

In case you think I have done something wrong then let me quote from a
copy of "Digital Technical Journal" entitled "Analysis of Data
Compression in the DLT2000 Tape Drive"  -- I think it may be relevant
:^)

"... The DECsystem 5500 system running the dd utility achieved write
rates of approx 0.85MB/s for the data types. Running tapex utility's
performance test (which is not disk or file system limited) on a similar
machine resulted in rates of more than 3MB/s..."

Also (very revealing) -

"To maximise the applicability of the tests results, we wanted to use
"real world" data. To do so in our test environment was not practical or
would have introduced delays between blocks, thus ruining any throughput
measurements."

I would ask that manufacturers put "real world" performance data on their
advertising.

However the drives offer high capacity and higher throughput rates than
either Exabyte or DAT and we will definately be bying a number of
drives!

I do not have experience of NSR and guess (from what I have heard) that
this would increase throughput. However this costs real money so should
not be a pre-requisite. (yes I know that V3.2 of OSF/1 comes with a
single server licence).

Would anyone from DEC care to comment on the performance (throughput) of
the filesystem and whether this will improve. Does this limitation apply
to all i/o (for example disk) ??

Your mileage may vary.


Dave...
