> would talk to the server and we could make the software tolerant of 
> being disconnected from the network while it is moved. We doubt that a 
> standard diskless configuration that swaps across the network would
> tolerate the network going away. We need to hide the network recovery 
> from the users.
> 
> Does anyone know if this is possible with DEC OSF/1 V3.0? Any
> assistance would be greatly appreciated.
> 
> Thanks in advance.
> 
> John Birrell
> CIMlogic Pty Ltd
> 
I'm not sure about booting diskless, but it is certainly possible
to run DEC OSF/1 V3.0 without any swapfile.  I'm writing this reply
on a system configured that way.  The only gotcha is that the system
must have enough memory so that it *never* tries to page out.  If it
does, processes will start dying and your system may crash.

To run like this, you must do two things:
	1) Remove all swap entries from /etc/fstab
	2) Remove the file link /etc/swapdefault

The change will take effect when the system boots.  I can't think
of anyway to make the standard system installation scripts do
this -- they insist on having at least one swap partition.
Just 'cause I work for Digital doesn't mean that this is a supported
configuration;  as far as I know the system documentation doesn't
even mention it.

