Dr. Andrew E. Pollard (pollard@tatanka.bmen.tulane.edu) wrote:
: Hello:

: We have a dual processor Alpha, but I can't figure out how to:

: 	1. determine the processor I'm running my job on
: 	2. compile a FORTRAN code to run across both processors

: I looked for the FAQ for this newgroup because I thought I might
: find this information in that source, but I could not find the FAQ.

: Could someone point me to the right places in either the bookreader or
: the man pages for parallel prosessing?

: Thanks in advance ... Andy Pollard

In order to get a program to take advantage of your 2 CPUs,  you'll have to 
use "threads".  This is a way to create different threads of execution that
can be scheduled on the different CPUs.  This is sort-of like forking separate
processes from within your code, but these "threads" are all under the same 
PID.  If you don't break your code into multiple threads, you'll always
run on a single CPU.  In fact,  OSF/1's scheduler schedules threads, not
processes.  Look up "pthreads" (Posix threads) in the man pages or bookreader.  
