In answer to your query, yes OSF/1 comes with all the basic TCP/IP
communication tools and utilities that you'd expect in a standard Unix
offering, specifically included are DNS, BIND, NIS (aka YP), EGP, FTP, NFS,
Telnet, TFTP, Finger, SNMP, SMTP, SLIP and CSLIP.  The OS add-ons are in the
following categories:

- Server Extensions: Supports Remote Installation Service (RIS)

- Developers' Toolkit:  designed for programers using languages other than C
and includes a more functional version of dbx for source code debugging.

- C Developers Extensions:  complete development environment for both the base
and worksystem environment, including the full function version of dbx

Then there is the usual assortment of layered products.

FWIW, Digital argues that OSF/1 IS Unix...

Tom Herendeen


