You mostly answer this yourself later on......

|>
|>	As far as I can see, you can load and install subsets
|>from NFS mounted disks, with no additional requirements in
|>principle. This has the advantage of allowing to load directly
|>from a remote CD-ROM thus saving a RIS area and its burdens...

Not sure exactly what those burdens are - especially if you link to the CDROM
instead of telling RIS to extract the software into a RIS area.  Of course, if
you use RIS, then everyone knows where to access software from.  Actually, all
they need to know is the server name.  Proper registration takes care of making
the appropriate RIS area and products available.  Doing it the other way you
suggest is fine.  You'll just have to communicate to everyone which host and
where on the host the software is.  Controlling access to the software is more
difficult that way.

|>
|>	So, what is it that is special to RIS and not NFS?
|>Besides requiring you to copy the products to disk and requiring
|>a RIS area on it which seems more like a hindrance, what is the
|>advantage of RIS?

You are incorrect if you believe that it is a requirement to copy the products
to disk.  You have the option to do so or to have the RIS area linked to the
CD-ROM.

|>
|>	Or is it only the possibility of controlling which 
|>software subsets a client has access to (a finer grained
|>control)? Allowing full OS installations in machines with
|>no previous OS?

Two important differences!  Especially the second.

|>
|>	Though I have not tried it, I have the impression that
|>you can even update the operating system from an NFS mounted
|>file system. So, is it there something I'm missing? Any clue?
|>

As long as your network is running in single user mode and you can mount the
remote file system, you probably can do an update.  Haven't tried this myself.

RIS does three main things:  it acts as a software repository, it provides 
client registration services, and it provides boot services.  What you are
doing provides only the first thing (software repository).  If this is 
sufficient to meet your needs - terrific!

Dave


