X-Newsreader: TIN [version 1.2 PL2]

This is an FYI for those who are looking to put their RC startup variables
in both /etc/rc.config and /etc/rc.config.site. The short answer is: DONT!

The program rcmgr does a good job of managing these files.  For background,
rcmgr will first look in /etc/rc.config.site, if the variable isn't found
there then it will look in /etc/rc.config.  This would be a nice feature for
us since we could put host specific stuff in rc.config (like hostname) and
generic site stuff in rc.config.site.

Unfortunately some startup scripts in /sbin/init.d do not follow standards
and do not use rcmgr to get its needed startup variables.  Specifically,
look in the files /sbin/init.d/inet (starts network, sets hostname) or 
/sbin/init.d/nfs and you will see a line like this:

if [ -f /etc/rc.config ] ; then
        . /etc/rc.config

Which means that they are dot executing the rc.config file bypassing rcmgr
altogether. A good example of following this standard can be found in the
file /sbin/init.d/named:

BIND_CONF=`rcmgr get BIND_CONF`
BIND_SERVERTYPE=`rcmgr get BIND_SERVERTYPE`
BIND_SERVERARGS=`rcmgr get BIND_SERVERARGS`

Hey OSF/1 developers, GET IT TOGETHER AND FIX THIS!

--
Larry Cannell (cannell@ped.pto.ford.com)
Powertrain Electronics
Ford Motor Company


