>In article: <3gm9im$ok0@amhux3.amherst.edu>  pdchapin@unix.amherst.edu (PAUL D. CHAPIN) writes:
>> 
>> When I try to login to the system using a LAT connection, I get the
>> following:
>> 
>> 
>> DEC OSF/1 Version V3.0 (amhux4.amherst.edu) 638
>> 
>> login: pdchapin
>> Cannot obtain database information on this terminal
>>                                                              
>> This would appear to be a simple matter of updating a database to make
>> sure that it includes a reference for whatever is being reported to
>> it.  Unfortunately, the info on what login is actually doing is a
>> little sparse.  Anybody have an suggestions?
>> 
>> BTW, when I connect without using LAT, I don't have any problem.
>> 
>
>Try adding:-
>
>*:t_devname=*:chkent:
>
>in /etc/auth/system/ttys
>
>This should work for V2.0, can't speak for V3.0

It won't help if he's using the STREAMS lat devices /dev/lat/nnn
(as recommended by DEC) rather than the BSD ones /dev/ttynn. That's
because the "*" wildcard in /etc/auth/system/ttys only applies to
device files in /dev itself, not in subdirectories. Similarly, with
the SysV pseudoterminals /dev/pts/nnn, you need separate entries for
all of those. Oh, and separate entries for all X terminals that want
to connect via xdm too. Since we have 200 LAT terminal devices, 738
ptys, a couple of hundred X terminals (but potentially thousands),
it makes for rather large ttys and devassign files. This was reported
to DEC in the middle of last July while we field-tested 3.0 but they
downgraded the problem. For the SysV LAT devices, add lines like
    lat123:t_devname=lat123:chkent:
*and* add a corresponding line
    lat123:v_devs=/dev/lat/123:v_type=terminal:chkent:
to /etc/auth/system/devassign. Repeat for every LAT device you run a
getty on and every /dev/pts people might use in a telnet/rlogin session.

--Malcolm
