EDT+ is nice because it integrates into other tools available from BBC and on
the interoperability tools CD-ROM, namely, VCL and Vmail.  Vmail has a 1-up
vs OpenVMS' MAIL utility:  you can switch into the editor even after you've
begun to compose a new message or reply.  If you remember the original
implementation of EDT on VMS, EDT+ has a very similar feel (for example, you
must use the ENTER key to terminate commands), although BBC has added features.

If people really are talking about "TPU with an EDT keypad" rather than EDT,
and they took advantage of TPU's capabilities to create a new section file
with extensions to the editor, they might be more interested in nu/TPU from
a/Soft.  nu/TPU is a full TPU implementation with section files, multiple
keypads and a Motif version.  It's not free, and not on the i14y tools CD-ROM,
but it can feel really good for your fingers to be in a familiar editor.
